SELECT 'core-2026-09-v4','past-perfect','87b630c771d79eac83e3be02f06e226cf9455e201ca4d4e9ebd574542396373d',payload || '{"slug":"past-perfect","title":"Past perfect: the earlier past","level":"B1","order":32,"summary":"The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","guidance":"Read the form and meaning together. Study the contrasts, then explain a worked example in your own words before starting a fresh practice session.","learningGoals":["Connect two points in time","Build the perfect verb","Negatives and questions","Time expressions and short answers"],"rules":[{"title":"Connect two points in time","explanation":"The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","pattern":"Subject + **had / had + past participle**","commonMistakes":["Before and after can already show the sequence, so past simple is sometimes enough. Past perfect is not required merely because an event happened a long time ago."],"examples":["Use the past perfect: She **had prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Use the past perfect: She **had restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Build the perfect verb","explanation":"A perfect construction uses **have as an auxiliary**, followed by a past participle. Regular participles normally end in -ed. Irregular ones must be learned with their base and past forms: write / wrote / written. Do not substitute an -ing form unless you also build the continuous construction.","pattern":"She **had written**. / They **had written**.","commonMistakes":["The form written is a participle; wrote is past simple. They are not interchangeable after have."],"examples":["Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Put **not after had or had**. For a yes/no question, move the auxiliary before the subject. The participle stays unchanged. There is no extra do or did because the perfect construction already contains an auxiliary.","pattern":"Had they finished? / They **had not finished**.","commonMistakes":["Do not combine did with a perfect auxiliary to make its negative or question."],"examples":["Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they prepared the visitor guide before the inspection began?","Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they restored the old portrait before the inspection began?"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Time expressions and short answers","explanation":"Read the whole sentence to locate the reference time. Here it is **before the inspection began**. Keep the appropriate form of have in a short answer and omit the repeated participle. Since names a starting point; for names a duration. Neither word automatically selects a single tense.","pattern":"Yes, they **had**. / No, they **had not**.","commonMistakes":["Been can describe a completed visit; gone often means the person has left and has not yet returned."],"examples":["Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began. (prepare)","Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **restored** the old portrait before the inspection began. (restore)"],"skill":"application"}],"tips":[],"source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"chapter":{"introduction":["The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Connect two points in time","explanation":"The past perfect looks backwards from a past reference point. It marks the earlier event with had + past participle. This is useful when the narrative moves out of chronological order. The later event usually uses past simple. Once the order is clear, a story does not need past perfect in every sentence.","pattern":"Subject + **had / had + past participle**","commonMistakes":["Before and after can already show the sequence, so past simple is sometimes enough. Past perfect is not required merely because an event happened a long time ago."],"examples":["Use the past perfect: She **had prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Use the past perfect: She **had restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"form","id":"past-perfect-foundation-1","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Build the perfect verb","explanation":"A perfect construction uses **have as an auxiliary**, followed by a past participle. Regular participles normally end in -ed. Irregular ones must be learned with their base and past forms: write / wrote / written. Do not substitute an -ing form unless you also build the continuous construction.","pattern":"She **had written**. / They **had written**.","commonMistakes":["The form written is a participle; wrote is past simple. They are not interchangeable after have."],"examples":["Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began.","Make a past-perfect negative: They **had not restored** the old portrait before the inspection began."],"skill":"meaning","id":"past-perfect-foundation-2","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Negatives and questions","explanation":"Put **not after had or had**. For a yes/no question, move the auxiliary before the subject. The participle stays unchanged. There is no extra do or did because the perfect construction already contains an auxiliary.","pattern":"Had they finished? / They **had not finished**.","commonMistakes":["Do not combine did with a perfect auxiliary to make its negative or question."],"examples":["Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they prepared the visitor guide before the inspection began?","Begin this past-perfect question: **Had** they restored the old portrait before the inspection began?"],"skill":"contrast","id":"past-perfect-foundation-3","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"title":"Time expressions and short answers","explanation":"Read the whole sentence to locate the reference time. Here it is **before the inspection began**. Keep the appropriate form of have in a short answer and omit the repeated participle. Since names a starting point; for names a duration. Neither word automatically selects a single tense.","pattern":"Yes, they **had**. / No, they **had not**.","commonMistakes":["Been can describe a completed visit; gone often means the person has left and has not yet returned."],"examples":["Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **prepared** the visitor guide before the inspection began. (prepare)","Choose the participle for the past perfect: We had **restored** the old portrait before the inspection began. (restore)"],"skill":"application","id":"past-perfect-foundation-4","domain":"verbs","referenceIds":["bc-past-perfect"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v3"},{"domain":"verbs","id":"narrative-time-frames","title":"Narrative sequencing and optional past perfect","explanation":"Past perfect locates a situation before a past reference point, especially when the story moves backwards. Past simple can continue a sequence once the order is established. Before and after can make the order clear without past perfect. Narrative present is another stylistic way to tell events, provided the perspective remains coherent.","pattern":"She arrived, but he **had left**.","examples":["After she **finished**, she went home.","He opened the box. Inside, someone **had hidden** a note."],"commonMistakes":["Do not keep every earlier event in past perfect throughout an entire story. Once the earlier time frame is established, past simple often carries the narrative."],"referenceIds":["cambridge-comprehensive-scope"],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"}],"recap":["Subject + **had / had + past participle**","She **had written**. / They **had written**.","Had they finished? / They **had not finished**.","Yes, they **had**. / No, they **had not**."],"readingVersion":"core-2026-09-v4"},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["present-perfect-continuous"],"readingReferenceIds":["bc-past-perfect","cambridge-comprehensive-scope"]}'::jsonb FROM grammar.content_catalog_topics WHERE version='core-2026-09-v3' AND slug='past-perfect'
